Problem Statement
You are a traveler on a 2D grid of size m × n.
You start at the top-left corner (m, n) and your goal is to reach the bottom-right corner (1, 1).

Movement Rules
You may move Down, which decreases m by 1

You may move Left, which decreases n by 1

No other moves are allowed

Restrictions
You must use recursion only

You are not allowed to use any external data structures such as arrays, lists, or dictionaries

Task
Write a recursive function that returns the total number of unique paths from (m, n) to (1, 1) following the allowed movements.

Edge Case Considerations
If m = 1 and n = 1, the starting cell is the destination, so there is exactly one valid path. 
If m = 1 and n > 1, only left moves are possible, so there is exactly one unique path. 
If m > 1 and n = 1, only downward moves are possible, so there is exactly one unique path. 
If m ≤ 0 or n ≤ 0, the grid is invalid and the number of paths is 0.

  solution: 
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int uniquePaths(int m, int n) {
    // Invalid grid
    if (m <= 0 || n <= 0)
        return 0;

    // Destination reached
    if (m == 1 && n == 1)
        return 1;

    // Recursive case
    return uniquePaths(m - 1, n) + uniquePaths(m, n - 1);
}

int main() {
    int m, n;
    cin >> m >> n;
    cout << uniquePaths(m, n);
    return 0;
}
